<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<appendix xml:id="appendix-prereqs-top">
<title>Prerequisites: Topology</title>
<introduction>

<p>
	We briefly cover some key ideas about topology. 
	In particular, we will focus on the topology on <m>\mathbb{R}</m>.
</p>
</introduction>
<section>
<title>Open and closed sets in <m>\mathbb{R}</m></title>
<definition>
	<statement>
		<p>A subset <m>A \subseteq \mathbb{R}</m> is <term>open</term> if for all <m>x\in A</m> there exists <m>r&gt;0</m> such that <m>(x-r, x+r) \subseteq A</m>.</p>
		<p>A subset <m>B \subseteq \mathbb{R}</m> is <term>closed</term> if its complement <m>\mathbb{R}\backslash B</m> is open.
		<idx><h>open set</h></idx>
		<idx><h>closed set</h></idx>
		</p>
	</statement>
</definition>


<definition>
	<statement>
		<p>Let <m>X \subseteq \mathbb{R}</m>.
			A set <m>A \subseteq X</m> is <term>open in <m>X</m></term> if there is an open subset <m>A' \subseteq \mathbb{R}</m> such that <m>A = A' \cap X</m>.
			Similarly, a set <m>B \subseteq X</m> is <term>closed in <m>X</m></term> if there is a closed subset <m>B \subseteq \mathbb{R}</m> such that <m>B = B' \cap X</m>.
			<idx><h>open set</h></idx><idx><h>closed set</h></idx></p>
	</statement>
</definition>
<p>
	The following theorem lists some key properties of open sets.
</p>	

<theorem xml:id="thm-open-sets">
	<statement>
		<p>
			<ol>
			<li><m>\emptyset,\ \mathbb{R}</m> are open;
			</li>
			<li>if <m>\mathcal{F}</m> is any collection of open sets then 
				<md> \bigcup_{F\in \mathcal{F}} F \text{ is open}; </md>
			</li>
			<li>if <m>A_1,\ldots, A_n</m> are open then 
				<md> \bigcap_{i=1}^n A_i \text{ is open.} </md>
			</li>
			</ol>
		</p>
	</statement>
</theorem>

<example>
<p>We list here some examples of open set, closed sets, sets which are both open and closed, and sets which are neither.
<ol>
<li><m>\emptyset</m> and <m>\mathbb{R}</m> are open and closed.
</li>
<li><p>Open intervals are open, i.e. intervals of the form <m>(-\infty, b),\ (a,+\infty)</m> and <m>(a,b)</m>.</p>
</li>
<li>Closed intervals are closed, i.e. intervals of the form <m>(-\infty, b],\ [a,+\infty)</m> and <m>[a,b]</m>.
</li>
<li>Finite subsets of <m>\mathbb{R}</m> are closed.
</li>
<li>The rational numbers <m>\mathbb{Q}</m> are neither open nor closed.
</li>
<li>The intervals <m>(a,b]</m> and <m>[a,b)</m> are neither open nor closed.
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</example>


<remark>
<p>Using <xref ref="thm-open-sets"/> and <xref ref="eq-de-morgan" text="custom">DeMorgan's identities</xref>, we can show that an arbitrary <em>intersection</em> of closed sets is closed, and a finite <em>union</em> of closed sets is closed.</p>
</remark>

<!--<p>A useful characterization of closed sets in <m>\mathbb{R}</m> is the following.</p>
<proposition xml:id="prop-closed">
<p>A subset <m>B \subseteq \mathbb{R}</m> is closed if and only if given any convergent sequence <m>(x_n)_n</m> of <m>\mathbb{R}</m> with <m>x_n \in B</m> for all <m>n</m>, we must have <m>\lim x_n \in B</m>.</p>
</proposition>-->

<definition>
	<notation>
		<usage><m>\overline{A}</m></usage>
	  <description>closure of <m>A</m></description>
	</notation>
	<notation>
		<usage><m>A^\circ</m></usage>
	  <description>interior of <m>A</m></description>
	</notation>
<p>Let <m>A\subseteq \mathbb{R}</m>.
The <term>closure</term> of <m>A</m>, denoted <m>\overline{A}</m> is the smallest closed set containing <m>A</m>.
The <term>interior</term> of <m>A</m>, denoted <m>A^\circ</m>, is the largest open set contained in <m>A</m>.
A subset <m>A\subseteq \mathbb{R}</m> is <term>dense</term> in <m>\mathbb{R}</m> if <m>\overline{A}=\mathbb{R}</m>.</p>
<p>If <m>X \subseteq \mathbb{R}</m> and <m>A \subseteq X</m>, then the <term>closure of <m>A</m> in  <m>X</m></term> is the set <m>\overline{A} \cap X</m>. Similarly, the <term>interior of <m>A</m> in <m>X</m></term> is the set <m>A^\circ \cap X</m>.
A set <m>A\subseteq X</m> is <term>dense in <m>X</m></term> if the closure of <m>A</m> in <m>X</m> is all of <m>X</m>.
<idx><h>closed set</h><h>closure</h></idx>
<idx><h>open set</h><h>interior</h></idx>
</p>
</definition>

<example>
<p>The rational numbers <m>\mathbb{Q}</m> are dense in <m>\mathbb{R}</m>.
The irrational numbers <m>\mathbb{R}\backslash \mathbb{Q}</m> are also dense in <m>\mathbb{R}</m>.</p>
</example>

<p>We make frequent use of the following property of open sets in this course.</p>

<proposition xml:id="prop-open-sets">
	<statement>
		<p>
		Every non-empty open set in <m>\mathbb{R}</m> is the disjoint union of countably many open intervals.			
		</p>
	</statement>
	<proof>
<p>Let <m>A\subseteq \mathbb{R}</m> be non-empty open set.
Take <m>x\in A</m>.
Since <m>A</m> is open, there exists <m>y&gt;x</m> such that <m>(x,y) \subseteq A</m>.
Similarly, there exists <m>z&lt;x</m> with <m>(z,x) \subseteq A</m>.
Let
<md>
\begin{aligned}
a_x &amp;= \inf \{z \colon (z,x) \subseteq A\} \\
	b_x &amp;= \sup \{y \colon (x,y) \subseteq A\}
\end{aligned}
</md>
(allowing for the possibility that <m>a_x = -\infty</m> and <m>b_x = +\infty</m>).
Let <m>I_x = (a_x, b_x)</m>.
We will show that <m>I_x\subseteq A</m> but <m>a_x,b_x \notin A</m>.</p>
<p>Take <m>w\in I_x</m>. Suppose <m>x&lt;w&lt;b_x</m>.
By definition of <m>b_x</m> as the supremum, there exists <m>y&gt;w</m> such that <m>(x,y) \subseteq A</m>.
Thus <m>w\in A</m>.
Similarly, if <m>w&lt; x</m> we get <m>w\in A</m>.
Hence <m>I_x \subseteq A</m>.</p>
<p>Now assume <m>b_x \in \mathbb{R}</m> and <m>b_x\in A</m>. 
Then, since <m>A</m> is open , there is an <m>r&gt;0</m> such that <m>(b_x-r, b_x+r) \subseteq A</m>.
But then <m>(x, b_x+r)\subseteq A</m>.
This contradicts the definition of <m>b_x</m> as a supremum, and hence <m>b_x\notin A</m>.
Similarly, <m>a_x\notin A</m>.</p>
<p>Take <m>x,y \in A</m>.
Note that, either <m>I_x = I_y</m> or <m>I_x \cap I_y= \emptyset</m>.
Hence the collection <m>\{I_x \colon x\in A\}</m> is a disjoint collection of open intervals.
Further, since <m>x\in I_x</m> for all <m>x\in A</m>, we have
<md> A = \bigcup_{x\in A} I_x. </md>
It remains to show that there are countably many distinct intervals <m>I_x</m>.
Note that, since <m>\mathbb{Q}</m> is dense in <m>\mathbb{R}</m>, each open interval <m>I_x</m> contains rational numbers.
Thus, the set <m>\{I_x\colon x\in A\}</m> can be indexed by a subset of <m>\mathbb{Q}</m>. 
Since <m>\mathbb{Q}</m> is countable this indexing set is countable, and we are done.</p>
</proof>
</proposition>

<definition>
<p>A countable intersection of open sets if called a <m>G_\delta</m>-set.
A countable union of closed sets is called an <m>F_\sigma</m>-set.</p>
<idx><h sortby="G-delta set"><m>G_\delta</m>-set</h></idx>
<idx><h sortby="F-sigma set"><m>G_\delta</m>-set</h></idx>
</definition>
<remark>
<p>The term <m>G_\delta</m> comes from German. The German for area is <sq>Gebeit</sq>, and the German for intersection is <sq>Durschnitt</sq>. Hence a <m>G_\delta</m> set comes from an intersection of areas.</p>
<p>The term <m>F_\sigma</m>, on the other hand, comes from another language altogether. In French the word for closed is <sq>fermé</sq>, and the word for sum is <sq>somme</sq>. Hence an <m>F_\sigma</m> set is a sum of closed sets.</p>
</remark>

<subsection><title>Open sets in <m>\bR^n</m></title>
<p>
	In <m>\bR^n</m> there is also a natural way to define open sets. 
	Heuristically, an open set is one which does not contain any points in its boundary.
	In order to define an open set, we need to first understand what we mean by distance in <m>\bR^n</m>.
	In <m>\bR</m>, we use the absolute value to determine the distance between two numbers; in <m>\bR^n</m> we use the Euclidean-norm (or <m>2</m>-norm).
	For <m>x = (x_1,\ldots,x_n)</m> and <m>y = (y_1,\ldots,y_n)</m> in <m>\bR^n</m> we define the distance between <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> as
	<md>
		\|x - y\|_2= \left(\sum_{i=1}^n(x_i-y_i)^2 \right)^{1/2}.
	</md>
	Note that, for <m>n = 1, 2, 3</m>, this value is the length of the straight line from <m>x</m> to <m>y</m>.
</p>

<definition>
	<statement>
		<p>
			A set <m>U \subseteq \bR^n</m> is <term>open</term> if for all <m>x \in U</m> there exists <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m> such that
			<md>
				\{y \in \bR^n \colon \|x-y\|_2\lt 2 \} \subseteq U.
			</md>
			A set <m>F \subseteq \bR^n</m>	 is <term>closed</term> if <m>\bR^n \backslash F</m> is open.
			<idx><h>open set</h></idx><idx><h>closed set</h></idx>
		</p>		
	</statement>
</definition>

<p>
	Most of what was said for open sets in <m>\bR</m> is true for open sets in <m>\bR^n</m> when <m>n \geq 2</m>.
	In particular, the analogous statement of <xref ref="thm-open-sets"/> holds in <m>\bR^n</m>; the interior and closure of a set is defined analogously; and <m>F_\sigma</m> and <m>G_\delta</m> sets are similarly defined.
	We warn that <xref ref="prop-open-sets"/> does not translate in its entirety to <m>\bR^n</m>. 
	Define an <term>open ball</term> in <m>\bR^n</m> to be a set of the form
	<md>
		\{y \in \bR^n \|x-y\|_2\lt r\}
	</md>
	for a fixed <m>x \in \bR^n</m> and fixed <m>r \gt 0.</m>
	It is true that every open set in <m>\bR^n</m> is a countable union of open balls.<fn>You can use the the fact that <m>\bQ^n</m> is dense in <m>\bR^n</m> to prove this.</fn>
	However, we can not assert that every open set is a countable union of <em>disjoint</em> open balls.
</p>	
</subsection>
</section>



<section>
<title>Metric spaces and General topology</title>

<p>
	The definition of open sets in <m>\bR</m> and <m>\bR^n</m> relies on being able to define a distance between between: <m>|x-y|</m> for <m>x,y \in \bR</m>, <m>\|x-y\|_2</m> for <m>x,y\in \bR^n</m>.
	If we have a <sq>distance</sq> (which is called a <term>metric</term>) on a set <m>X</m> then we can define open sets in <m>X</m>.
</p>	

<definition>
	<notation>
		<usage><m>d(x,y)</m></usage>
	  <description>metric</description>
	</notation>
	<notation>
		<usage><m>(X,d)</m></usage>
	  <description>metric space</description>
	</notation>
	<statement>
		<p>
			Let <m>X</m> be a non-empty set.
			A function <m>d \colon X\times X \rightarrow \bR</m> is a <term>metric</term> if
			<ol>
				<li>
					<p>
						<m>d(x,y) \geq 0</m> for all <m>x,y \in X</m>, and <m>d(x,y)=0</m> if and only if <m>x=y</m>;
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<m>d(x,y) = d(y,x)</m> for all <m>x,y \in X</m>; and
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<m>d(x,y) \leq d(x,z) + d(z,y)</m> for all <m>x,y,z \in X</m> (<term>triangle inequality</term>).<idx><h>triangle inequality</h></idx>
					</p>
				</li>
			</ol>
			<idx><h>metric</h></idx>
			<idx><h>metric space</h></idx>
			If <m>d</m> is a metric on <m>X</m>, the pair <m>(X,d)</m> is called a <term>metric space</term>.
			When the metric <m>d</m> is clear, we often simply call <m>X</m> a metric space.
		</p>
	</statement>
</definition>

<definition>
	<statement>
		<p>
			Let <m>(X,d)</m> be a metric space.
			A set <m>U \subseteq X</m> is <term>open</term> if for all <m>x \in X</m> there exists <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m> such that 
			<md>
				\{ y\in X \colon d(x,y)\lt \varepsilon \} \subseteq U.
			</md>
			A set <m>F \subseteq X</m> is closed if <m>X \backslash F</m> is open.
			<idx><h>open set</h></idx><idx><h>closed set</h></idx>
		</p>
	</statement>
</definition>

<p>
	Let <m>(X,d)</m> be a topological space.
	If <m>A \subseteq X</m>, the <term>closure</term> of <m>A</m>, denoted by <m>\overline{A}</m>, is the smallest closed set containing <m>A</m>; and
	the <term>interior</term> of <m>A</m>, denoted by <m>A^\circ</m>, is the largest open set contained in <m>A</m>.
	A set <m>A \subseteq X</m> is <term>dense</term> if <m>\overline{A} = X</m>.
	A set <m>A</m> is <term>dense</term> in <m>X</m> if <m>\overline{A} = X</m>.
	<idx><h>closed set</h><h>closure</h></idx>
	<idx><h>open set</h><h>interior</h></idx>
	<idx><h>dense set</h></idx>
</p>

<p>
	In a metric space, open sets have many of the same properties that open sets in <m>\bR</m> have.
</p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-open-sets-metric">
	<statement>
		<p>
			Let <m>(X,d)</m> be a metric space. The open sets in <m>(X,d)</m> have the following properties:
			<ol>
			<li><m>\emptyset,\ X</m> are open;
			</li>
			<li>if <m>\mathcal{F}</m> is any collection of open sets then 
				<md> \bigcup_{F\in \mathcal{F}} F \text{ is open}; </md>
			</li>
			<li>if <m>A_1,\ldots, A_n</m> are open then 
				<md> \bigcap_{i=1}^n A_i \text{ is open.} </md>
			</li>
			</ol>
		</p>
	</statement>
</theorem>

<example>
	<p>
		The absolute value function on <m>\bR</m> defines a metric: the distance between two numbers <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> is <m>|x-y|</m>.
		For <m>n\geq 2</m>, the Euclidean distance between two points <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> is <m>\|x-y\|_2</m>.
		This defines a metric on <m>\bR^n</m>. That triangle inequality is satisfied is not immediate; it follows from <xref ref="exer-2-norm"/>.
	</p>
</example>

<p>
	In a topological space, we look at open sets in a set without reference to a distance.
	<xref ref="thm-open-sets"/> and <xref ref="thm-open-sets-metric"/> inspire the following definition
</p>

<definition>
	<notation>
		<usage><m>(X,\tau)</m></usage>
	  <description>topological space</description>
	</notation>
	<statement>
		<p>
			A topology on a non-empty set <m>X</m> is a collection of subsets <m>\tau</m> of <m>X</m> with the properties
			<ol>
			<li><m>\emptyset</m>, <m>X \in \tau</m>;
			</li>
			<li>if <m>\mathcal{F}</m> is a collection of sets in <m>\tau</m> then <m>\bigcup_{F\in \mathcal{F}} F \in \tau</m>;
			</li>
			<li>if <m>F_1, \ldots, F_n \in \tau</m> then <m>\bigcap_{i=1}^n F_i \in \tau</m>.
			</li>
			</ol>
			We call the elements of <m>\tau</m> <term>open sets</term>.
			If <m>A</m> is open, we call <m>X\backslash A</m> <term>closed</term>.
			We call <m>(X,\tau)</m>, or simply <m>X</m> if the topology <m>\tau</m> is clear, a <term>topological space</term>.
			<idx><h>topology</h></idx>
			<idx><h>topological space</h></idx>
			<idx><h>open set</h></idx>
			<idx><h>closed set</h></idx>
		</p>
	</statement>
</definition>
<definition>
	<statement>
		<p>
			Let <m>(X,\tau)</m> be a topological space, and let <m>A \subseteq X</m>.
			The <term>closure</term> of <m>A</m>, denoted <m>\overline{A}</m>, is the smallest closed set containing <m>A</m>; and the <term>interior</term> of <m>A</m>, denoted <m>A^\circ</m>, is the largest open set containing <m>A</m>.
		</p>
	</statement>
</definition>


<example>
	<p>
		Let <m>(X,d)</m> be a metric space, and let <m>\tau</m> be all open sets in <m>X</m> (with respect to the metric <m>d</m>).
		Then <m>\tau</m> is a topology on <m>X</m> by <xref ref="thm-open-sets-metric"/>.
	</p>
</example>


<example>
	<p>
		Let <m>X</m> be any non-empty set and let <m>\tau = \P(X).</m>
		Then <m>(X,\tau)</m> is a topological space.
		We call <m>\tau</m> the <term>discrete topology</term> on <m>X</m>.<idx><h>topology</h><h>discrete topology</h></idx>
	</p>
</example>

<example>
	<p>
		Let <m>X</m> be any non-empty set and let <m>\tau = \{\emptyset, X\}.</m>
		Then <m>(X,\tau)</m> is a topological space.
		We call <m>\tau</m> the <term>trivial topology</term> on <m>X</m>.
		The trivial topology is also refered to as the <term>indiscrete topology.</term>
		<idx><h>topology</h><h>trivial topology</h></idx>
		<idx><h>topology</h><h>indiscrete topology</h><see>trivial topology</see></idx>
	</p>
</example>

<p>
	Let <m>(X,\tau)</m> be a topological space, and let <m>A \subseteq X</m> be a non-empty subset.
	Define
	<md>
		\tau_A = \{U \cap A \colon U \in \tau\}.
	</md>
	Then <m>(A,\tau_A)</m> is a topological space. 
	This topology on <m>A</m> is called the <term>induced topology</term> or <term>relative topology</term> on <m>A</m>.
	<idx><h>topology</h><h>induced topology</h></idx>
	<idx><h>topology</h><h>relative topology</h><see>induced topology</see></idx>
</p>



<definition xml:id="def-haus">
	<statement>
		<p>
			A topological space <m>(X,\tau)</m> is <term>Hausdorff</term>
			if given <m>x,y \in X</m> with <m>x \neq y</m>, there are disjoint open sets <m>U_x,U_y \in \tau</m> with <m>x \in U_x</m> and <m>y \in U_y</m>.<idx><h>topology</h><h>Hausdorff</h></idx>
		</p>
	</statement>
</definition>

<example>
	<p>
		Every metric space is a Hausdorff topological space.
	</p>
</example>

<example>
	<p>
		If <m>X</m> is a set with more than one point, and <m>\tau = \{\emptyset, X\}</m> is the trivial topology on <m>X</m>, then <m>(X,\tau)</m> is not a Hausdorff topological space.
	</p>
</example>

<remark>
	<p>
		The property of being <em>Hausdorff</em> is an example of a <term>separation axiom</term> on a topological space. The Hausdorff property is sometimes denoted by <m>T_2</m>.
		There are several other separation axioms, but we will not concern ourselves with them.
	</p>
</remark>
<!-- </section>
<section>
<title>Compactness</title> -->
<definition>
<p>Let <m>(X,\tau)</m> be a topological space and let <m>A \subseteq X</m>.
An <term>open cover of <m>A</m></term> is a collection of open sets <m>\mathcal{F} \subseteq \tau</m> such that
<md>
A \subseteq \bigcup_{F\in\mathcal{F}} F.
</md>
An open cover <m>\mathcal{F}</m> of <m>A</m> has a <term>finite subcover of <m>A</m></term> if there is a finite subset <m>\{F_1,\ F_2,\ldots,F_n\} \subseteq \mathcal{F}</m> such that
<md> A \subseteq F_1 \cup F_2 \cup \ldots \cup F_n. </md>
A set <m>A \subseteq X</m> is called <term>compact</term> if given any open cover <m>\mathcal{F}</m> of <m>A</m>, <m>\mathcal{F}</m> has a finite subcover of <m>A</m>.
<idx><h>compact</h></idx>
<idx><h>open cover</h></idx>
<idx><h>open cover</h><h>subcover</h></idx>
</p>
</definition>

<p>The following theorem gives an easy characterization of compact sets in <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m>.
<fn>Proofs are easy to find. The wikipedia page for the result provides several <xref ref="HeiBor"/>.</fn></p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-heine-borel">
<title>Heine-Borel Theorem</title>
<statement>
	<p>
		<idx><h>Heine-Borel Theorem</h></idx>
		A subset <m>A \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n</m> is compact if and only if <m>A</m> is closed and bounded.
	</p>
</statement>
<!-- <proof>
<p>We will first show that a closed bounded interval <m>[a,b]</m> is compact.
If <m>a=b</m>, then <m>[a,b]</m> is finite and clearly compact.
Assume <m>a&lt;b</m>.
Let <m>\mathcal{F}</m> be an arbitrary open cover of <m>[a,b]</m>.
Note that, for any <m>a\leq x\leq b</m>, <m>\mathcal{F}</m> is also an open cover of <m>[a,x]</m>.
Let
<md> E = \{x\in [a,b] \colon \mathcal{F} \text{ has a finite subcover of }[a,x]\}. </md>
Note that <m>[a,a]= \{a\}</m>.
As <m>\mathcal{F}</m> is an open cover of <m>[a,b]</m> there is an open set <m>F \in \mathcal{F}</m> such that <m>a\in F</m>.
Hence <m>a\in E</m>.</p>
<p>As <m>E</m> is a non-empty, bounded subset of <m>\mathbb{R}</m> we can take its supremum.
Let
<md> c = \sup E. </md>
Clearly, <m>a&lt; c\leq b</m>.
If we can show that <m>c=b</m> then we are done.</p>
<p>Now, since <m>c\in [a,b]</m>, there is an open set <m>G \in \mathcal{F}</m> such that <m>c\in G</m>.
As <m>G</m> is open, there exists <m>r&gt;0</m> such that <m>(c-r,c+r)\subseteq G</m>.
As <m>c-r</m> is not an upper bounded for <m>E</m>, there exists <m>x\in E</m> such that <m>c-r&lt;x\leq c</m>.
By definition of <m>E</m>, there are open sets <m>F_1, \ldots, F_n \in \mathcal{F}</m> such that
<md> [a,x] \subseteq F_1 \cup F_2 \cup \ldots \cup F_n. </md>
Thus,
<md> [a, c+r) \subseteq F_1 \cup F_2 \cup \ldots \cup G. </md>
It follows that <m>c=b</m>, otherwise we could find <m>x\in [a,b]</m> with
<md> a\leq c&lt; x &lt; c+r </md>
and have <m>[a,x]</m> finitely covered by <m>F_1,\ldots,F_n,G</m>; contradicting the definition of <m>c</m> as the supremum of <m>E</m>.
We have now shown that <m>[a,b]</m> is compact.</p>
<p>Let <m>A</m> be an arbitrary closed bounded subset of <m>\mathbb{R}</m> and let <m>\mathcal{F}</m> be any open cover of <m>A</m>.
As <m>A</m> is bounded there is a closed interval <m>[a,b]</m> such that <m>A \subseteq [a,b]</m>.</p>
<p>Let <m>A^c = \mathbb{R}\backslash A</m> be the complement of <m>A</m>.
Since <m>A</m> is closed, <m>A^c</m> is open.
Let <m>\mathcal{F}' = \mathcal{F} \cup \{A^c\}</m>.
Then <m>\mathcal{F}'</m> is an open cover of <m>[a,b]</m>.
Since <m>[a,b]</m> is compact, <m>\mathcal{F}'</m> has a finite subcover of <m>[a,b]</m>.
That is
<md> A \subseteq [a,b] \subseteq F_1 \cup \ldots \cup F_n, </md>
for some <m>F_1,\ldots,F_n \in \mathcal{F}'</m>.
If <m>F_1,\ldots, F_n \in \mathcal{F}</m>, then we have a finite subcover of <m>A</m> from open sets in <m>\mathcal{F}</m>, and we are done.
Otherwise one of <m>F_1,\ldots,F_n</m> is equal to <m>A^c</m>.
Without loss of generality, say <m>F_1 = A^c</m>.
Then we have
<md> A \subseteq F_2 \cup \ldots \cup F_n </md>
and so <m>F_2, \ldots, F_n</m> form a finite open subcover of <m>A</m> from <m>\mathcal{F}</m>.
Thus <m>A</m> is compact.</p>

<p>
Now suppose that <m>A \subseteq \bR^n</m> is closed and bounded, with <m>n \geq 2</m>.
Let <m>\F</m> be an open cover of <m>A</m>.
Assume also, that if <m>B \subseteq \bR^k</m> is closed and bounded, and <m>k\lt n</m>, that <m>B</m> is compact.
For each <m>x \in \bR</m> let
<md number="no">
	A_x = \{ (a_2,\ldots,a_n)\in \bR^{n-1} \colon (x,a_2,\ldots,a_n)\in A\}.
</md>

</p>
</proof> -->
</theorem>
<!-- 
<definition>
<p>A set <m>A \subseteq \mathbb{R}</m> is called <term>sequentially compact</term> if every infinite sequence of elements of <m>A</m> has a subsequence which converges to an element of <m>A</m>.</p>
</definition>
<p>The Bolzano-Weierstrass theorem, together with <xref ref="prop-closed" text="Proposition"/>, tells us that a set <m>A\subseteq \mathbb{R}</m> is sequentially compact if and only if it is closed and bounded. Combining the Bolzano-Weierstrass Theorem and the Heine-Borel Theorem we get the following result.</p>
<corollary xml:id="cor-heine-borel">
<p>Let <m>A\subseteq \mathbb{R}</m>. Then the following are equivalent:</p>
<ol>
<li><p><m>A</m> is compact;</p>
</li>
<li><p><m>A</m> is sequentially compact;</p>
</li>
<li><p><m>A</m> is closed and bounded.</p>
</li>
</ol>
</corollary>
<p>Many statements which are true for continous real-valued functions on closed bounded intervals, are in fact true for real-valued functions on closed bounded sets, using similar proofs (which rely on compactness/sequential compactness). We record some of these facts below:</p> -->

<remark>
<p>In a general metric space, a set being closed and bounded is not necessarily equivalent to being  compact.
	If you study a proof of the Heine-Borel theorem in <m>\bR^n</m>, it will rely on the fact that <m>n</m> is finite.
	In infinite dimensional normed vector spaces there are closed and bounded sets which are not compact.
</p>
</remark>

</section>

<section><title>Continuity and convergence</title>
<p>
	Recall the following definitions from a first course in real analysis.
	Let <m>A \subseteq \bR</m> be a non-empty set and let <m>f \colon A \rightarrow \bR</m> be a function.
	The function <m>f</m> is <term>continuous</term> at a point <m>x_0 \in A</m> if for all <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m> there exists a <m>\delta \gt 0</m> such that <m>|f(x_0)-f(y)|\lt\varepsilon</m> for all <m>y \in A</m> with <m>|x_0-y|\lt \delta.</m>
	The function <m>f</m> is <term>continuous</term> if <m>f</m> is continuous at every point in <m>A</m>.
	The function <m>f</m> is <term>uniformly continuous</term> if for all <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m> there is a <m>\delta \gt 0</m> such that if <m>x,y \in A</m> and <m>|x-y| \lt \delta</m> then <m>|f(x) - f(y)|\lt \delta</m>.
	Note that uniform continuity is a stronger condition than continuity.
	<idx><h>continuous function</h><h>at a point in <m>\bR</m></h></idx>
	<idx><h>continuous function</h></idx>
	<idx><h>continuous function</h><h>uniformly continuous</h></idx>
	<idx><h>convergent sequence</h></idx>
</p>

<p>
	All of these definitions can be easily adapted to apply to general metric spaces.
</p>
<definition>
	<statement>
		<p>
				Let <m>(X,d_X)</m> and <m>(Y,d_Y)</m> be two metric spaces, and let <m>f \colon X \rightarrow Y</m> be a function.
				The function <m>f</m> is <term>continuous</term> at a point <m>x_0 \in X</m> if for all <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m> there exists a <m>\delta \gt 0</m> such that <m>d_Y(f(x_0),f(y))\lt\varepsilon</m> for all <m>y \in X</m> with <m>d_X(x_0,y)\lt \delta.</m>
				The function <m>f</m> is <term>uniformly continuous</term> if for all <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m> there is a <m>\delta \gt 0</m> such that if <m>x,y \in X</m> and <m>d_X(x,y) \lt \delta</m> then <m>d_Y(f(x),f(y))\lt \delta</m>.
				<idx><h>continuous function</h><h>on a metric space</h></idx>
				<idx><h>continuous function</h><h>uniformly continuous</h><h>on a metric space</h></idx>
		</p>
	</statement>
</definition>

<p>
	Similarly, the usual definition of convergence in <m>\bR</m> is easily adapted to the metric space case.	
</p>

<definition>
	<notation>
		<usage><m>\lim_{n}x_n = x_0</m></usage>
	  <description>limit of a sequence</description>
	</notation>
	<notation>
		<usage><m>x_n \rightarrow x_0 \text{ as }n\rightarrow \infty.</m></usage>
	  <description>limit of a sequence</description>
	</notation>
	<statement>
		<p>
			Let <m>(X,d)</m> be a metric space and let <m>(x_n)_{n}</m> be a sequence in <m>X</m>.
			The sequence <m>(x_n)_n</m> converges to a point <m>x_0 \in X</m> if for all <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m> there is a natural number <m>N</m> such that <m>d(x_n,x_0)\lt \varepsilon</m> when <m>n \geq N</m>.
			When <m>(x_n)_n</m> converges to <m>x_0</m> we call <m>x_0</m> the <term>limit</term> of the sequence <m>(x_n)_n</m>.
			We denote this by <m>\lim_{n\rightarrow \infty}x_n = x_0,</m>, <m>\lim_{n}x_n = x_0,</m>, or
			<md>
				x_n \rightarrow x_0 \text{ as }n\rightarrow \infty.
			</md>
			<idx><h>limit</h></idx>
			<idx><h>convergent sequence</h></idx>
		</p>
	</statement>
</definition>

<p>
	We record two other characterizations of continuity, one based on open sets, and one based on sequences.
</p>

<proposition>
	<statement>
		<p>
			Let <m>(X,d_X)</m> and <m>(Y,d_Y)</m> be two metric spaces, and let <m>f \colon X \rightarrow Y</m> be a function.
			The function <m>f</m> is continuous if and only if whenever <m>(x_n)_n</m> is a sequence in <m>X</m> converging to <m>x_0 \in X</m>, the sequence <m>(f(x_n))_n</m> in <m>Y</m> converges to <m>f(x_0).</m>
		</p>
	</statement>
</proposition>

<proposition xml:id="prop-cont-open">
	<statement>
		<p>
			Let <m>(X,d_X)</m> and <m>(Y,d_Y)</m> be two metric spaces, and let <m>f \colon X \rightarrow Y</m> be a function.
			The function <m>f</m> is continuous if and only if <m>f^{-1}(U)</m> is an open set in <m>X</m> for all open sets <m>U \subseteq Y</m>.
		</p>
	</statement>
</proposition>

<p>
	Sequences can also be used to characterize closed sets.
</p>

<proposition xml:id="prop-closed">
	<statement>
		<p>
			Let <m>(X,d)</m> be a metric space and let <m>A \subseteq X</m>.
			Then <m>A</m> is closed if and only if whenever <m>(x_n)_n</m> is a convergent sequence in <m>X</m> with each <m>x_n \in A</m>, then <m>\lim_n x_n \in A.</m>
		</p>
	</statement>
</proposition>

<p>
	There are times when a continuous function is automatically <em>uniformly continuous</em>.
</p>

<proposition xml:id="prop-compact-cont">
	<statement>
		<p>
			Let <m>(X,d_X)</m> and <m>(Y,d_Y)</m> be metric spaces. 
			Further suppose that <m>X</m> is compact.
			Let <m>f \colon X \rightarrow Y</m> be a continuous function.
			Then, <m>f(X)</m> is a compact subset of <m>Y</m> and <m>f</m> is uniformly continuous.
			
		</p>
	</statement>
</proposition>

<corollary xml:id="cor-compact-cont">
	<statement>
		<p>
			Let <m>(X,d)</m> be a compact metric space and let <m>f \colon X \rightarrow \bR</m> be a continuous function.
			Then <m>f</m> is uniformly continuous and <m>f</m> attains its maximum and minimum values.
		</p>
	</statement>
</corollary>

<p>
	In general topological spaces we can also define continuity of functions and convergence of sequences.
	It turns out that sequences do not always play the same role in topological spaces as they do in metric spaces.
	The correct analogue of a convergent sequence is (the more general) object of a convergent <term>net</term>.<fn>There are other objects called <term>filters</term> that can be used instead of nets.</fn>
	As we can avoid using nets in these notes, we will not discuss them.
	We will, however, need to talk about continuity.
	Without having a metric, the <m>\varepsilon-\delta</m> definition of continuity does not readily translate to arbitrary topological spaces.
	However, we do have open sets.
	<xref ref="prop-cont-open"/> motivates the following definition.
</p>

<definition>
	<statement>
		<p>
			Let <m>(X,\tau_X)</m> and <m>(Y,\tau_Y)</m> be two topological spaces.
			A function <m>f \colon X \rightarrow Y</m> is <term>continuous</term> if <m>f^{-1}(U) \in \tau_X</m> for all <m>U \in \tau_Y.</m>
			<idx><h>continuous function</h><h>on a topological space</h></idx>
		</p>
	</statement>
</definition>

<p>
	As hinted above, the direct analogue of <xref ref="prop-closed"/> does not hold in general topological spaces.
	However, <xref ref="prop-compact-cont"/> (and <xref ref="cor-compact-cont"/>) do translate readily topological spaces.
</p>

<proposition xml:id="prop-top-compact-cont">
	<statement>
		<p>
			Let <m>(X,\tau_X)</m> and <m>(Y,\tau_Y)</m> be topological spaces. 
			Further suppose that <m>X</m> is compact.
			Let <m>f \colon X \rightarrow Y</m> be a continuous function.
			Then, <m>f(X)</m> is a compact subset of <m>Y</m> and <m>f</m> is uniformly continuous.
			
		</p>
	</statement>
</proposition>

<corollary xml:id="cor-top-compact-cont">
	<statement>
		<p>
			Let <m>(X,\tau_X)</m> be a compact topological space and let <m>f \colon X \rightarrow \bR</m> be a continuous function.
			Then <m>f</m> is uniformly continuous and <m>f</m> attains its maximum and minimum values.
		</p>
	</statement>
</corollary>
</section>

</appendix>